.\" raztest(1) - build and run the tests of a raz program
.Dd October 22, 2026
.Dt RAZTEST 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm raztest
.Nd build and run the tests of a raz program
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl I Ar dir
.Op Fl j Ar n
.Op Fl t Ar secs
.Op Fl o Ar dir
.Op Fl r Ar text
.Op Fl w
.Op Fl \-tap
.Op Fl \-junit Ar file
.Op Fl \-seed Ar n
.Op Fl \-fuzz Ar secs
.Ar file.raz Ns | Ns Ar dir ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
builds each test with
.Ic raz -Werror ,
runs it, and says which passed.
A test is a module whose
.Ic main
is its test: it passes if it builds and then exits with status 0, as a
program that checks with the library's
.Ic Check
does when no check fails.
.Pp
The tests are the files given.
A directory gives each
.Pa *.raz
directly in it that has a line starting
.Ql proc main* ,
in the order of their names.
A test's name is its file's, without
.Pa .raz ;
two tests may not share one.
.Pp
Each test is built into the
.Fl o
directory as
.Ar name ,
and run there with standard input from
.Pa /dev/null ,
in a process group of its own.
What the compiler writes, and then what the test writes on standard
output and standard error, goes to
.Ar name Ns .out
beside it, which stays for a look afterwards.
A build or a run that takes longer than the
.Fl t
seconds has its process group killed.
.Pp
As each test ends,
.Nm
writes a line for it, in the order the tests were given: the word
.Ql ok ,
.Ql FAIL
or
.Ql TIMEOUT ,
the file, for a failure what failed,
such as
.Ql doesn't compile ,
.Ql status 1
or
.Ql signal 11 ,
and the seconds it took to build and run.
Under a failure comes its
.Pa .out ,
indented by four spaces.
Last comes a summary:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
12 passed, 1 failed, 0 timed out, 3.41s
.Ed
.Pp
The three words are in colour when standard output is a terminal.
.Pp
With
.Fl \-tap
standard output is TAP version 14 instead: the lines
.Ql TAP version 14
and
.Ql 1..N ,
then for each test
.Ql ok K - file
or
.Ql not ok K - file # status 1
(the same reason as the
.Ql FAIL
line, or
.Ql timed out ) ,
with a failure's
.Pa .out
under it as comment lines starting with
.Ql # ,
and the summary as a last comment line.
There is no colour.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--junit file"
.It Fl I Ar dir
Look for modules in
.Ar dir
too, as
.Ic raz -I
does.
It may be given more than once.
.It Fl j Ar n
Build and run
.Ar n
tests at once; 1 if not given.
.It Fl t Ar secs
Kill a build, or a run, that takes longer than
.Ar secs
seconds; 60 if not given.
.It Fl o Ar dir
Put the programs and their
.Pa .out
files in
.Ar dir ,
made if it isn't there;
.Pa build/raztest
if not given.
.It Fl r Ar text
Run only the tests whose name holds
.Ar text .
.It Fl w , Fl \-watch
Run the tests, then run them all again whenever a file changes, until
interrupted.
Watched are the
.Pa *.raz
files directly in the directory of each test file given, in each
directory given, and in each
.Fl I
directory, not those below them.
The files' times and sizes are looked at every 300 ms, and a change is
waited out until the files stay as they are for one look, since an
editor writes in steps.
All the tests run again, not only those a change reaches.
The operands are read again each time, so a new test file is found.
On a terminal the screen is cleared before each run, and
.Ql watching N files; ^C stops
is written after the summary.
With
.Fl \-junit
the file is written again after each run.
.It Fl \-tap
Write TAP instead of the usual lines.
.It Fl \-junit Ar file
Besides the usual output, write the results to
.Ar file
as JUnit XML, which CI services read: one
.Ql testcase
for each test, its
.Ql classname
the file, with a
.Ql failure
holding the log for one that failed or timed out.
The directory of
.Ar file
must exist; if it can't be written, the status is 2.
Control bytes XML 1.0 forbids in a log are written as
.Ql \&? ;
invalid UTF-8 is passed through.
.It Fl \-seed Ar n
For tests that use the library's
.Ic Prop :
set
.Ev PROP_SEED
to
.Ar n ,
a whole number from 0, so that their fresh cases are those of an
earlier run, which a failing property names.
.It Fl \-fuzz Ar secs
For tests that use
.Ic Prop :
set
.Ev PROP_TIME
to
.Ar secs ,
so that each property is tried on fresh cases for that many seconds,
not on 100 of them.
It raises
.Fl t
to
.Ar secs
+ 60, unless
.Fl t
is given.
.It Fl h , Fl \-help
Say the options, and exit.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "NO_COLOR"
.It Ev RAZ
The compiler; if unset,
.Ic raz
on the
.Ev PATH .
.It Ev NO_COLOR
If set and not empty, no colour.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 if every test passed; 1 if one failed or timed out; 2 for a mistake
in the command line, a file that isn't there, or an output directory
that can't be made.
Stopped by an interrupt or a hangup, it kills the tests under way and
exits with 128 and the signal's number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run a project's tests, four at a time:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
raztest -j 4 -I src test
.Ed
.Pp
In CI, with a report for the service to show:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
raztest -j 4 -I src --junit build/junit.xml test
.Ed
.Pp
Fuzz each property of the tests in
.Pa test
for ten minutes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
raztest -I src --fuzz 600 test
.Ed
.Pp
A Makefile's check that uses
.Nm
where it is installed, and its own loop where it isn't:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
check:
	@if command -v raztest >/dev/null; then raztest -I src test; \e
	else fail=0; for f in test/*.raz; do \e
	    raz -Werror -I src -o build/t $$f && build/t || fail=1; \e
	done; test $$fail = 0; fi
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr raz 1 ,
.Xr raz-lib 3
.Sh BUGS
.Fl j
doesn't default to the number of processors, which the library can't
tell yet.
